tempered Semitone. But in Pythagorean temperament there were two Semitones, the small 90 and the large 114 cents, and the rule was to make the Semitone closest to the note to which it led, thus *c* 114 *c*♯ 90 *d*, *d* 114 *d*♭ 90 *d*. And this notation was retained @@A0@@
>>>
<<<
@@A0@@
===
even in meantone temperament, where the relations were reversed, as *c* 76 *c*♯ 117 *d*, *d* 76 *d*♭ 117 *c*; but practically this made no difference except to the singer, as the player had only one Semitone at command. This writing is still continued in equal @@A0@@
>>>
<<<
@@A0@@
===
temperament, although the two Semitones are now equalised as 100 cents, thus *c* 100 *c*♯ 100 *d*, and *d* 100 *d*♭ 100 *c*. But in just intonation we have Semitones of various dimensions, *c* 114 *c*♯ 90 *d*, *c* 112 *d¹*♭ 92 *d*, *c* 92 *c₁*♯ 112 *d*, *c* 90 *d*♭ 114 @@A0@@
>>>
